    ($SATQ$b1b5300b$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$025856-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Probability and conditional probability$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference"><table class="table_WithBorder tcp-b88bb88e-7c5f-4529-a9a6-333cc7c7bf27"><caption>Prices of 14 Different Cars</caption><tbody><tr><td class="tcp-66d20239-c8b4-4d3f-a9b5-f4802c9707cd">Type of car</td><td class="tcp-46d4bd36-f9b3-442c-b686-fd0d55f5450d">Priced at no more<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class="tcp-8df36ac7-65e1-433b-bb21-14ef0c0757d8">Priced greater<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class="tcp-3e8abc78-bdbb-4b2c-8f3b-bf6c46da1a01">Total</td></tr><tr><td class="tcp-f3bdf422-4bef-4c74-b8ba-4a2b99980336">Nonhybrid</td><td class="tcp-5dcba8ae-46e4-4148-95c6-72882f147e4e">5</td><td class="tcp-837091ea-415f-4312-8317-d92ba7709e86">3</td><td class="tcp-132ebab9-6994-478e-b2e1-fea47af2f182">8</td></tr><tr><td class="tcp-1a010d3e-e5ac-4cad-91fa-d3bd69d53148">Hybrid</td><td class="tcp-f51231ca-3bd9-4229-854f-530c094efdd7">2</td><td class="tcp-a9ff2060-f86a-46f1-bbad-badaa972e6c4">4</td><td class="tcp-0aac33fc-e7f1-4024-ba44-34b1fa45c91f">6</td></tr><tr><td class="tcp-f4abb043-9640-444b-b6e6-98ea05c0c68f">Total</td><td class="tcp-e01280ba-13c6-4437-9c2e-43f50a69aa1f">7</td><td class="tcp-afd593b0-8373-44c5-b89b-439d5e3e865f">7</td><td class="tcp-6cee79c3-6256-4c0b-bc04-048cfb273aa0">14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="item_stem">The table above shows information about 14 cars listed for sale on an auto dealership&rsquo;s website. If one of the cars listed for sale is selected at random, what is the probability that the car selected will be a hybrid car priced at no more than $25,000 ?</div>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_000_431ed921.png\" alt=\"one seventh\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_001_9f6de3a8.png\" alt=\"two sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_002_7445a232.png\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_003_d2577e54.png\" alt=\"four sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are 2 hybrid cars priced at no more than $25,000. It&rsquo;s also given that there are 14 cars total for sale. Therefore, the probability of selecting a hybrid priced at no more than $25,000 when one car is chosen at random is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_004_0b18d12b.png" alt="2 over 14 equals one seventh"></span>.<p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car priced greater than $25,000 when choosing one car at random. Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability, when choosing randomly from only the hybrid cars, of selecting one priced at no more than $25,000. Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car when selecting at random from only the cars priced greater than $25,000.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 5, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"b1b5300b","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"025856-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.E.","skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference\"><table class=\"table_WithBorder tcp-b88bb88e-7c5f-4529-a9a6-333cc7c7bf27\"><caption>Prices of 14 Different Cars</caption><tbody><tr><td class=\"tcp-66d20239-c8b4-4d3f-a9b5-f4802c9707cd\">Type of car</td><td class=\"tcp-46d4bd36-f9b3-442c-b686-fd0d55f5450d\">Priced at no more<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class=\"tcp-8df36ac7-65e1-433b-bb21-14ef0c0757d8\">Priced greater<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class=\"tcp-3e8abc78-bdbb-4b2c-8f3b-bf6c46da1a01\">Total</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-f3bdf422-4bef-4c74-b8ba-4a2b99980336\">Nonhybrid</td><td class=\"tcp-5dcba8ae-46e4-4148-95c6-72882f147e4e\">5</td><td class=\"tcp-837091ea-415f-4312-8317-d92ba7709e86\">3</td><td class=\"tcp-132ebab9-6994-478e-b2e1-fea47af2f182\">8</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-1a010d3e-e5ac-4cad-91fa-d3bd69d53148\">Hybrid</td><td class=\"tcp-f51231ca-3bd9-4229-854f-530c094efdd7\">2</td><td class=\"tcp-a9ff2060-f86a-46f1-bbad-badaa972e6c4\">4</td><td class=\"tcp-0aac33fc-e7f1-4024-ba44-34b1fa45c91f\">6</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-f4abb043-9640-444b-b6e6-98ea05c0c68f\">Total</td><td class=\"tcp-e01280ba-13c6-4437-9c2e-43f50a69aa1f\">7</td><td class=\"tcp-afd593b0-8373-44c5-b89b-439d5e3e865f\">7</td><td class=\"tcp-6cee79c3-6256-4c0b-bc04-048cfb273aa0\">14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n","stem":"<div class=\"item_stem\">The table above shows information about 14 cars listed for sale on an auto dealership&rsquo;s website. If one of the cars listed for sale is selected at random, what is the probability that the car selected will be a hybrid car priced at no more than $25,000 ?</div>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_000_431ed921.png\" alt=\"one seventh\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_001_9f6de3a8.png\" alt=\"two sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_002_7445a232.png\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_003_d2577e54.png\" alt=\"four sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are 2 hybrid cars priced at no more than $25,000. It&rsquo;s also given that there are 14 cars total for sale. Therefore, the probability of selecting a hybrid priced at no more than $25,000 when one car is chosen at random is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b1b5300b/img_004_0b18d12b.png\" alt=\"2 over 14 equals one seventh\"></span>.<p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car priced greater than $25,000 when choosing one car at random. Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability, when choosing randomly from only the hybrid cars, of selecting one priced at no more than $25,000. Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car when selecting at random from only the cars priced greater than $25,000.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":5,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959620,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"b1b5300b","skill_cd":"Q.E.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","createDate":1691007959620,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"025856-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"058c9d2b-2937-42a4-8e36-6da7a43124bf","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"025856-DC","section":"Math","body":"<div class=\"stimulus_reference\"><table class=\"table_WithBorder tcp-b88bb88e-7c5f-4529-a9a6-333cc7c7bf27\"><caption>Prices of 14 Different Cars</caption><tbody><tr><td class=\"tcp-66d20239-c8b4-4d3f-a9b5-f4802c9707cd\">Type of car</td><td class=\"tcp-46d4bd36-f9b3-442c-b686-fd0d55f5450d\">Priced at no more<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class=\"tcp-8df36ac7-65e1-433b-bb21-14ef0c0757d8\">Priced greater<br>&nbsp;than $25,000</td><td class=\"tcp-3e8abc78-bdbb-4b2c-8f3b-bf6c46da1a01\">Total</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-f3bdf422-4bef-4c74-b8ba-4a2b99980336\">Nonhybrid</td><td class=\"tcp-5dcba8ae-46e4-4148-95c6-72882f147e4e\">5</td><td class=\"tcp-837091ea-415f-4312-8317-d92ba7709e86\">3</td><td class=\"tcp-132ebab9-6994-478e-b2e1-fea47af2f182\">8</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-1a010d3e-e5ac-4cad-91fa-d3bd69d53148\">Hybrid</td><td class=\"tcp-f51231ca-3bd9-4229-854f-530c094efdd7\">2</td><td class=\"tcp-a9ff2060-f86a-46f1-bbad-badaa972e6c4\">4</td><td class=\"tcp-0aac33fc-e7f1-4024-ba44-34b1fa45c91f\">6</td></tr><tr><td class=\"tcp-f4abb043-9640-444b-b6e6-98ea05c0c68f\">Total</td><td class=\"tcp-e01280ba-13c6-4437-9c2e-43f50a69aa1f\">7</td><td class=\"tcp-afd593b0-8373-44c5-b89b-439d5e3e865f\">7</td><td class=\"tcp-6cee79c3-6256-4c0b-bc04-048cfb273aa0\">14</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n","prompt":"<div class=\"item_stem\">The table above shows information about 14 cars listed for sale on an auto dealership&rsquo;s website. If one of the cars listed for sale is selected at random, what is the probability that the car selected will be a hybrid car priced at no more than $25,000 ?</div>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one seventh\"></span></span></p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"two sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one third\"></span></span></p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"four sevenths\"></span></span></p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"a","rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that there are 2 hybrid cars priced at no more than $25,000. It&rsquo;s also given that there are 14 cars total for sale. Therefore, the probability of selecting a hybrid priced at no more than $25,000 when one car is chosen at random is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 over 14 equals one seventh\"></span>.<p>Choice B is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car priced greater than $25,000 when choosing one car at random. Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability, when choosing randomly from only the hybrid cars, of selecting one priced at no more than $25,000. Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of selecting a hybrid car when selecting at random from only the cars priced greater than $25,000.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959620,"updateDate":1691007959620}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959620, 1691007959620),
    ($SATQ$b2f6f17d$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$05323-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A customer&rsquo;s monthly water bill was $75.74. Due to a rate increase, her monthly bill is now $79.86. To the nearest tenth of a percent, by what percent did the amount of the customer&rsquo;s water bill increase?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p>4.1%</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>5.1%</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>5.2%</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>5.4%</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. To find the percent increase of the customer&rsquo;s water bill, the absolute increase of the bill, in dollars, is divided by the original amount of the bill, and the result is multiplied by 100%, as follows: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_000_79e2923f.png" alt="the fraction with numerator 79 point 8 6 minus 75 point 7 4, and denominator 75 point 7 4, is approximately equal to, 0 point 0 5 4;"></span>; <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_001_e5d3eb29.png" alt="0 point 0 5 4 times 100 percent, equals 5 point 4 percent"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice is the difference <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_002_812e10dd.png" alt="79 point 8 6, minus 75 point 7 4"></span> rounded to the nearest tenth, which is the (absolute) increase of the bill&rsquo;s amount, not its percent increase. Choice B is incorrect and may be the result of some calculation errors. Choice C is incorrect and is the result of dividing the difference between the two bill amounts by the new bill amount instead of the original bill amount.</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 3, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"b2f6f17d","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"05323-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.B.","skill_desc":"Percentages","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A customer&rsquo;s monthly water bill was $75.74. Due to a rate increase, her monthly bill is now $79.86. To the nearest tenth of a percent, by what percent did the amount of the customer&rsquo;s water bill increase?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p>4.1%</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p>5.1%</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p>5.2%</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p>5.4%</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. To find the percent increase of the customer&rsquo;s water bill, the absolute increase of the bill, in dollars, is divided by the original amount of the bill, and the result is multiplied by 100%, as follows: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_000_79e2923f.png\" alt=\"the fraction with numerator 79 point 8 6 minus 75 point 7 4, and denominator 75 point 7 4, is approximately equal to, 0 point 0 5 4;\"></span>; <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_001_e5d3eb29.png\" alt=\"0 point 0 5 4 times 100 percent, equals 5 point 4 percent\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice is the difference <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b2f6f17d/img_002_812e10dd.png\" alt=\"79 point 8 6, minus 75 point 7 4\"></span> rounded to the nearest tenth, which is the (absolute) increase of the bill&rsquo;s amount, not its percent increase. Choice B is incorrect and may be the result of some calculation errors. Choice C is incorrect and is the result of dividing the difference between the two bill amounts by the new bill amount instead of the original bill amount.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":3,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959629,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"b2f6f17d","skill_cd":"Q.B.","score_band_range_cd":5,"skill_desc":"Percentages","createDate":1691007959629,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"05323-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"961580fa-fa90-48b9-a47d-017d70f206b5","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"05323-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A customer&rsquo;s monthly water bill was $75.74. Due to a rate increase, her monthly bill is now $79.86. To the nearest tenth of a percent, by what percent did the amount of the customer&rsquo;s water bill increase?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p>4.1%</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p>5.1%</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p>5.2%</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p>5.4%</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. To find the percent increase of the customer&rsquo;s water bill, the absolute increase of the bill, in dollars, is divided by the original amount of the bill, and the result is multiplied by 100%, as follows: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction with numerator 79 point 8 6 minus 75 point 7 4, and denominator 75 point 7 4, is approximately equal to, 0 point 0 5 4;\"></span>; <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"0 point 0 5 4 times 100 percent, equals 5 point 4 percent\"></span>.<p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice is the difference <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"79 point 8 6, minus 75 point 7 4\"></span> rounded to the nearest tenth, which is the (absolute) increase of the bill&rsquo;s amount, not its percent increase. Choice B is incorrect and may be the result of some calculation errors. Choice C is incorrect and is the result of dividing the difference between the two bill amounts by the new bill amount instead of the original bill amount.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959629,"updateDate":1691007959629}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959629, 1691007959629),
    ($SATQ$b4912cc5$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$021593-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">The population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, increased from 2.5 in 1990 to 3.3 in 2014. During this time period, the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. By how many people did Iceland&rsquo;s population increase from 1990 to 2014?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">330,825</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">132,330</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">125,312</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">80,200</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. The increase in Iceland&rsquo;s population can be found by multiplying the increase in population density, in people per square kilometer, by the area, in square kilometers. It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland was 2.5 people per square kilometer in 1990 and 3.3 people per square kilometer in 2014. The increase in population density can be found by subtracting 2.5 from 3.3, which yields 0.8. It&rsquo;s given that the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. Thus, the increase in population is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_000_d2986bc9.png" alt="0 point 8 times 100,250"></span>, or 80,200.<p>Alternate approach: It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, in 1990 was 2.5. Since the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers, it follows that the population of Iceland in 1990 was <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_001_5fb8979b.png" alt="2 point 5 times 100,250"></span>, or 250,625. Similarly, the population of Iceland in 2014 was <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_002_65f31f05.png" alt="3 point 3 times 100,250"></span>, or 330,825. The population increase is the difference in the population from 1990 to 2014, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_003_66ad728e.png" alt="330,825 minus 250,625"></span>, which yields 80,200. Therefore, Iceland&rsquo;s population increased by 80,200 from 1990 to 2014.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the population of Iceland in 2014. Choice B is incorrect and may result from dividing 3.3 by 2.5, instead of subtracting 2.5 from 3.3. Choice C is incorrect and may result from dividing the population of Iceland in 1990 by 2.</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 4, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"b4912cc5","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"021593-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">The population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, increased from 2.5 in 1990 to 3.3 in 2014. During this time period, the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. By how many people did Iceland&rsquo;s population increase from 1990 to 2014?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">330,825</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">132,330</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">125,312</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">80,200</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The increase in Iceland&rsquo;s population can be found by multiplying the increase in population density, in people per square kilometer, by the area, in square kilometers. It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland was 2.5 people per square kilometer in 1990 and 3.3 people per square kilometer in 2014. The increase in population density can be found by subtracting 2.5 from 3.3, which yields 0.8. It&rsquo;s given that the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. Thus, the increase in population is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_000_d2986bc9.png\" alt=\"0 point 8 times 100,250\"></span>, or 80,200.<p>Alternate approach: It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, in 1990 was 2.5. Since the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers, it follows that the population of Iceland in 1990 was <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_001_5fb8979b.png\" alt=\"2 point 5 times 100,250\"></span>, or 250,625. Similarly, the population of Iceland in 2014 was <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_002_65f31f05.png\" alt=\"3 point 3 times 100,250\"></span>, or 330,825. The population increase is the difference in the population from 1990 to 2014, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/b4912cc5/img_003_66ad728e.png\" alt=\"330,825 minus 250,625\"></span>, which yields 80,200. Therefore, Iceland&rsquo;s population increased by 80,200 from 1990 to 2014.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the population of Iceland in 2014. Choice B is incorrect and may result from dividing 3.3 by 2.5, instead of subtracting 2.5 from 3.3. Choice C is incorrect and may result from dividing the population of Iceland in 1990 by 2.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":4,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959617,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"b4912cc5","skill_cd":"Q.A.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","createDate":1691007959617,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"021593-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"07a616f2-37d2-4820-99f9-b5bb2fbb6b4c","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"021593-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">The population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, increased from 2.5 in 1990 to 3.3 in 2014. During this time period, the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. By how many people did Iceland&rsquo;s population increase from 1990 to 2014?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">330,825</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">132,330</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">125,312</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph block_choice\">80,200</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. The increase in Iceland&rsquo;s population can be found by multiplying the increase in population density, in people per square kilometer, by the area, in square kilometers. It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland was 2.5 people per square kilometer in 1990 and 3.3 people per square kilometer in 2014. The increase in population density can be found by subtracting 2.5 from 3.3, which yields 0.8. It&rsquo;s given that the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers. Thus, the increase in population is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"0 point 8 times 100,250\"></span>, or 80,200.<p>Alternate approach: It&rsquo;s given that the population density of Iceland, in people per square kilometer of land area, in 1990 was 2.5. Since the land area of Iceland was 100,250 square kilometers, it follows that the population of Iceland in 1990 was <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 point 5 times 100,250\"></span>, or 250,625. Similarly, the population of Iceland in 2014 was <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"3 point 3 times 100,250\"></span>, or 330,825. The population increase is the difference in the population from 1990 to 2014, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"330,825 minus 250,625\"></span>, which yields 80,200. Therefore, Iceland&rsquo;s population increased by 80,200 from 1990 to 2014.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This is the population of Iceland in 2014. Choice B is incorrect and may result from dividing 3.3 by 2.5, instead of subtracting 2.5 from 3.3. Choice C is incorrect and may result from dividing the population of Iceland in 1990 by 2.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959617,"updateDate":1691007959617}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959617, 1691007959617),
    ($SATQ$b4f5a7ca$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$029581-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.G.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Evaluating statistical claims: Observational studies and experiments $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A survey was conducted using a sample of history professors selected at random from the California State Universities. The professors surveyed were asked to name the publishers of their current texts. What is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors in the United States</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors in the United States</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is correct. Selecting a sample at random when conducting a survey allows the results to be generalized to the population from which the sample was selected, but not beyond this population. In this situation, the population that the sample was selected from is history professors from the California State Universities. Therefore, the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized is all history professors at all California State Universities.<p>Choices A, B, and D are incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from history professors from the California State Universities, the results of the survey can&rsquo;t be generalized to all professors in the United States, all history professors in the United States, or all professors at all California State Universities. All three of these populations may use different texts and therefore may name different publishers.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"b4f5a7ca","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"029581-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.G.","skill_desc":"Evaluating statistical claims: Observational studies and experiments ","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A survey was conducted using a sample of history professors selected at random from the California State Universities. The professors surveyed were asked to name the publishers of their current texts. What is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors in the United States</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors in the United States</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Selecting a sample at random when conducting a survey allows the results to be generalized to the population from which the sample was selected, but not beyond this population. In this situation, the population that the sample was selected from is history professors from the California State Universities. Therefore, the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized is all history professors at all California State Universities.<p>Choices A, B, and D are incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from history professors from the California State Universities, the results of the survey can&rsquo;t be generalized to all professors in the United States, all history professors in the United States, or all professors at all California State Universities. All three of these populations may use different texts and therefore may name different publishers.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959626,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"b4f5a7ca","skill_cd":"Q.G.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Evaluating statistical claims: Observational studies and experiments ","createDate":1691007959626,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"029581-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"32f334c9-92fb-4cb6-8138-9752ffcb6ba2","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"029581-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A survey was conducted using a sample of history professors selected at random from the California State Universities. The professors surveyed were asked to name the publishers of their current texts. What is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors in the United States</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors in the United States</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All history professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">All professors at all California State Universities</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Selecting a sample at random when conducting a survey allows the results to be generalized to the population from which the sample was selected, but not beyond this population. In this situation, the population that the sample was selected from is history professors from the California State Universities. Therefore, the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized is all history professors at all California State Universities.<p>Choices A, B, and D are incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from history professors from the California State Universities, the results of the survey can&rsquo;t be generalized to all professors in the United States, all history professors in the United States, or all professors at all California State Universities. All three of these populations may use different texts and therefore may name different publishers.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959626,"updateDate":1691007959626}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959626, 1691007959626),
