$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">How many of the males surveyed responded that they do not play a school sport?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">109</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">252</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">468</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">688</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. The table summarizes all 1,000 responses from the students surveyed. If 312 are males who play a sport, 220 are females who play a sport, and 216 are females who do not play a sport, then 1,000 &ndash; 312 &ndash; 220 &ndash; 216 = 252 males who do not play a sport.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect. If 109 males who do not play a sport responded, then the table summary would be 109 + 312 + 220 + 216 = 857 total student responses rather than 1,000. If 468 males who do not play a sport responded, then the table summary would be 468 + 312 + 220 + 216 = 1,216 total student responses rather than 1,000. If 688 males who do not play a sport responded, then the table summary would be 688 + 312 + 220 + 216 = 1,436 total student responses rather than 1,000.<br>
&nbsp;</p></p>
